kernelbase: Redirect CreateFileA("ws2_32.dll") to the system32 copy (MapleStory / BlackCipher).

BlackCipher opens "ws2_32.dll" by bare name to read a clean on-disk image for
its integrity comparison.  The relative name resolves against the game's
working directory, where no such file exists under Wine, so the open fails and
the comparison can never succeed.

This is the second, unlabelled kernelbase hack found next to the .msf one in
Nexon's CrossOver-based FOSS tree (dlls/kernelbase/file.c, CreateFileA).  It
is kept separate from 0001 so the two can be bisected independently; only the
exact string "ws2_32.dll" is affected.

The NULL check matters: CreateFileA(NULL, ...) is not a crash upstream --
file_name_AtoW() feeds the pointer to RtlInitAnsiString(), which treats NULL as
an empty string, so the call just fails cleanly.  Comparing the bare pointer
before that would turn a harmless bad call from an application into a crash
inside kernelbase.

--- a/dlls/kernelbase/file.c
+++ b/dlls/kernelbase/file.c
@@ -755,6 +755,11 @@
     WCHAR *nameW;
 
     if ((GetVersion() & 0x80000000) && IsBadStringPtrA( name, -1 )) return INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE;
+
+    /* MapleStory HACK: BlackCipher opens ws2_32.dll by bare name; make that resolve to system32 */
+    if (name && !strcmp( name, "ws2_32.dll" ))
+        name = "C:\\windows\\system32\\ws2_32.dll";
+
     if (!(nameW = file_name_AtoW( name, FALSE ))) return INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE;
     return CreateFileW( nameW, access, sharing, sa, creation, attributes, template );
 }
